{
  "segments": [
    {
      "id": 140,
      "text": "New phone, download a new wallet app, type in those exact 12 words, and the algorithm perfectly rebuilds your"
    },
    {
      "id": 141,
      "text": "entire key structure and pulls your balance right back from the blockchain. But I mean, the flip side of that convenience is"
    },
    {
      "id": 142,
      "text": "extreme vulnerability, right? Oh, absolutely. Extreme vulnerability. If anyone else gets their hands on those 12 words..."
    },
    {
      "id": 143,
      "text": "They get all your money. Instantly. Irreversibly."
    },
    {
      "id": 144,
      "text": "They just type your words into their own phone and sweep your funds. This is why phishing is such a massive threat in this space."
    },
    {
      "id": 145,
      "text": "Hackers build malicious apps designed to look exactly like your real wallet. Yep. They flash a fake error on your screen that says,"
    },
    {
      "id": 146,
      "text": "you know, corrupted file, please verify your recovery code. But people need to know a legitimate, properly engineered wallet will"
    },
    {
      "id": 147,
      "text": "only ask for those words during the initial setup or if you are actively choosing to recover a lost wallet. Exactly. It will never"
    },
    {
      "id": 148,
      "text": "under any circumstances randomly prompt you to enter them. Wait, I need to stop you there for a second. Because everything I see in the news says"
    },
    {
      "id": 149,
      "text": "Bitcoin is this anonymous dark web shadow currency used by criminals. Right, that old stereotype. But you're telling me the blockchain is a"
    },
    {
      "id": 150,
      "text": "completely open public journal that everyone can see every transaction? Yes, anyone can see it. That sounds like"
    },
    {
      "id": 151,
      "text": "a privacy nightmare. It is a massive misconception. Bitcoin is not anonymous. It is pseudonymous."
    },
    {
      "id": 152,
      "text": "Pseudonymous? Right. Yes, the ledger is public. Anyone can view it. But when you want to receive funds, your"
    },
    {
      "id": 153,
      "text": "wallet generates a public address, a long string of letters and numbers. That address is your pseudonym."
    },
    {
      "id": 154,
      "text": "As long as your real-world identity isn't tied to that string of letters, your privacy is maintained. Here's where it gets really"
    },
    {
      "id": 155,
      "text": "interesting, though. Because if you reuse that same address over and over, well, that's where you run into trouble. Right."
    },
    {
      "id": 156,
      "text": "Say you put one single Bitcoin address on your website for donations, or you use the same address every time you invoice a client."
    },
    {
      "id": 157,
      "text": "Anyone who sends you money can just take that address, paste it into a public blockchain explorer, and see your entire"
    },
    {
      "id": 158,
      "text": "financial history. Exactly. It would be like handing a barista your credit card to buy a coffee and the barista suddenly being able to see your"
    },
    {
      "id": 159,
      "text": "entire bank statement, your total net worth, and everywhere else you shop that week. Which is terrifying. So, to maintain"
    }
  ]
}
